ATE, RUN FRESH AT THIS HEAD UNDER THIS CHAIN. Serialized behind /tmp/gitseq-docset.lock with no other go test running. git diff --check rc=0, go vet ./... rc=0, go build ./... rc=0, go test ./... -count=1 rc=0, every package ok, docset alone at 108s, and the three packages that failed at the first head all pass: cmd/gitseq-mcp 63.9s, internal/app 57.8s, internal/service 26.1s.\n\nEVIDENCE CARRIED FORWARD, AND WHY IT STILL HOLDS. The head has not moved, so these facts from the previous round describe this commit. The branch was rebased rather than rewritten, so a raw diff against the older approved head 7a9e94db is meaningless - it shows main's own progress. The branch contribution survived exactly: `git diff e677dd8f 5eb4e8b7` and `git diff 04c4a267 7a9e94db` are both 51,726 bytes, and after stripping only blob index lines and hunk line offsets the 1,192 remaining lines are byte-identical. All three commits carry Rests-On naming 69328046, which resolves at sequence 5256, kind request, neither retired nor stale.\n\nARCHITECTURE CONCLUSION. Layers touched, against docs/reference/architecture.md: Ordinary Git storage, internal/gitstore, untouched, no new storage primitive. Kernel, internal/kernel, contract changed and recorded in the same head - it gains an optional host-supplied opaque selector, gives it no authority, and performs no local checkpoint filesystem I/O, which the page now states in those words and which the code still satisfies. Application host and boundary adapter, internal/app, contract changed and recorded - it owns the bounded pointer file, the off switch and invalidation. CLI and documentation surface, cmd/gs and docs, one added command with its usage string, index entry and reference page. Composition and transport, internal/service, and the MCP surface, cmd/gitseq-mcp - contracts unchanged; both only adopt the application's invalidation seam in their tests, which is the correct direction of dependency and moves nothing across a boundary. The page matches reality at this head and the re-cut changed no layer contract.\n\nSECURITY CONCLUSION. No unresolved defect. Untrusted pointer and fallback behaviour is the heart of this change and it is right: neither selector is authority, so an attacker owning the pointer file chooses only which fully verified object is tried first, never what is accepted. Object-ID validation runs before the first Git command that touches a candidate, so an option-shaped value cannot reach git. Depth ordering is taken from unauthenticated payload and orders attempts only; a forged deeper candidate fails authentication and the loop falls through. The ref is written before the pointer in both the publication and the repair path, so a local persistence failure can neither strand a new checkpoint object unreachable nor block the recovery anchor. Every candidate still passes the whole identity, sequence, payload-binding, key-rotation and sequencer-signature path before its prefix is used, and a missing, malformed, tampered or profile-mismatched selector is only a cache miss that falls back to the ordinary full audit. I established these by execution in earlier rounds against this identical code - ten malformed pointer shapes and a directory rejected, a symlink replaced rather than written through with the target intact, mode 0600, garbage-collection reachability proved against git gc --prune=now, genesis validated before path construction on both entry points, and mutation proof that the canonical byte comparison and the size bound are load-bearing - and I did not re-run them, because tree identity establishes the code is byte-for-byte what those probes ran against. The restart behaviour in the two new areas is disclosure rather than trust, and the tests pin that the degraded paths say honestly which path they took and leak no custody. The one nit I raised and still do not block on is that the off switch matches the literal string \"off\", so GITSEQ_CHECKPOINT=false silently leaves checkpoints enabled.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION CONCLUSION. Nothing left to cut without weakening what matters. The bounds, the canonical validation, the invalidation lever and the host-owned filesystem seam all stay; each is load-bearing and I proved as much by mutation. The dual size bound is deliberate defence in depth on an untrusted-input path, with the test pinning the property rather than either line. The two artifacts added this round add no machinery at all - they only make the description of the head complete, which is the cheapest possible way to close the audit.\n\nLocal and origin tips both read 5eb4e8b7, the worktree is clean, and all six cited artifacts are live, non-stale, at the exact head and the implementer's own. This approval is for this exact head and nothing else; any change to it returns the work to fresh artifacts and a fresh verdict. I did not merge and did not edit the branch.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:17afb9de4b67cd09e6d8b6b476fc16fea3f5906d","head":"5eb4e8b756ed46986cb73f87109f31372ea6a849","verdict":"approved"}}